{{!
    @template enrol_apply/queue_chip

    One applied filter, named and removable.

    A partial of its own so that the markup exists ONCE. The server renders it in a loop when the
    page loads, and the queue's own module renders it through core/templates after an as-you-type
    refresh - because the refresh replaces the table's region alone and never re-renders the filter
    bar, so a chip row left as the server wrote it would describe a filter that is no longer
    applied. Two hand-written copies of this markup would be two things to keep in step, and the
    one that drifts is always the one nobody is looking at.

    The remove control is a real link and keeps a working href, so it survives with scripting off
    and gives the right address to a middle-click or a copied link; the module intercepts the
    click rather than replacing the element.

    Its label is REAL text in a visually-hidden span rather than an aria-label, and the glyph is
    hidden from assistive technology so the two are not announced together.

    One reason for that is measured and one is not, and they are worth keeping apart. The measured
    one: an aria-label is not findable by name, so no Behat step naming the control can click it -
    Mink's link selector matches an anchor's id, text, title, rel and image alt, and aria-label is
    on none of those paths. The scenario failed on exactly that. The unmeasured one, offered as a
    reason rather than a finding: hidden text is generally handled more consistently by assistive
    technology than an aria-label on a link, and it is translated by the same machinery as the
    rest of the interface. Nothing here tested a screen reader, and an earlier version of this
    docblock said "every screen reader" as though something had.

    Note the consequence for assertions. This hidden text is real page text, so it contains the
    filter's name and value a second time - "Remove the filter Search: goncalves" beside the
    chip's own "Search goncalves". A whole-page assertion has to pick wording that only one of
    them carries, which the colon does.

    Classes required for JS:
    * none - the module matches on data-region.

    Data attributes required for JS:
    * data-region="chip" on the wrapper, so the row can be emptied and refilled.
    * data-filter on the remove link, naming which filter it drops.

    Context variables required for this template:
    * filter string - the filter's name, as the table's filterset knows it
    * name string - what the filter is called, in the reader's language
    * value string - what it is set to
    * removeurl string - url of the same listing without this one filter
    * removelabel string - accessible wording of the remove control

    Example context (json): {
        "filter": "search",
        "name": "Search",
        "value": "goncalves",
        "removeurl": "https://example.com/enrol/apply/manage.php?id=140",
        "removelabel": "Remove the filter Search: goncalves"
    }
}}
<span class="enrol_apply-chip" data-region="chip">
    <span class="enrol_apply-chipname">{{name}}</span> {{value}}
    <a href="{{removeurl}}" class="enrol_apply-chipremove" data-filter="{{filter}}"><span aria-hidden="true">&#10005;</span><span class="visually-hidden">{{removelabel}}</span></a>
</span>
